  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery displays mostly clear skies with \nonly a few stray cirrus clouds drifting across the region. A \nbroad, tranquil weather pattern is firmly established, marking \nthe beginning of a prolonged period of quiet weather across \neastern Idaho. Predominantly zonal upper-level flow over the \nnext couple of days will maintain light surface winds and hold \ndaytime maximum temperatures right around seasonal \nclimatological norms. Lower-elevation valleys will top out in \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s through Wednesday, accompanied by \nclear, crisp overnight lows in the upper 30s to upper 40s.\n\nA transition toward unseasonable warmth develops during the \nsecond half of the workweek. Operational models and ensemble \nguidance agree that an upper-level ridge anchored over the \nsouthern Great Basin will amplify northward across the \nIntermountain West on Thursday. Strong subsidence and rising 500\nmb heights will drive daytime highs 5 to 10 degrees above \nseasonal averages. Valley locations across the Snake Plain and \nMagic Valley will widespread reach the upper 70s to lower 80s \nstarting Thursday, with similar warm readings persisting right \nthrough the weekend.\n\nHigh confidence continues for this ridge-dominated regime to\nremain locked in place through the first few days of October and\nlikely into the first full week of the month. Ensemble clusters\nshow no indication of any troughs or cold fronts capable of \nbreaking down the ridge. Consequently, dry conditions and above-\nnormal temperatures will prevail across the region, with no \ntrue fall-like weather or widespread precipitation expected \nanytime soon.",
